exports[`the text of the hub charts > writes every province and chart as one reviewed text, so a change in wording or in a figure shows in review > ca-ab 1`] = `
"takeHome
Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance together stop growing at $85,000, at most $5,770 a year. Employment Insurance stops growing at about $68,900, and the Canada Pension Plan at about $74,600. Above that, each extra dollar loses only income tax.
From $371,350 up, the line is straight: you keep 53% of every extra dollar. At $500,000 you keep 59.65% of your salary.

averageRate
Up to $2,000 the average is 0%. Employment Insurance starts just above $2,000 and counts for more as your salary rises, until the full rate applies by about $2,033. The average is about 1.6% from about $2,040.
Up to about $19,200 you pay no income tax, only Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance. The average is 5.5% at $10,000 and 7.1% at $20,000.
After that the average climbs: 12.9% at $30,000, 18.9% at $50,000, 22.7% at $70,000 and 25.5% at $100,000.
It climbs more slowly than the marginal rate, because each higher rate applies only to the dollars above its bracket. At $500,000 the average is 40.4%, while the top marginal rate is 47%.

marginalRate
Up to $2,000 the rate is 0%. From $2,000 to about $2,033 the rate is 100%, because the Employment Insurance counted rises by $1 for every $1 of salary, until it reaches about $33. After that it takes 1.63 cents of each extra dollar.
From $4,000 to about $19,200 the rate is 7.5%: Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance, with no income tax yet. It is 20.5% from $19,200, when federal income tax starts.
Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance take about 7.5 cents of each dollar. Employment Insurance stops at about $68,900, and the main Canada Pension Plan amount at $74,600. A second Canada Pension Plan tier takes 4 cents of each dollar from $74,600 to $85,000, so the rate falls less at $74,600 than at $85,000.
Alberta’s 8% tax starts at about $24,420. The rate is 28% from $24,420 to $59,070. Alberta has no tax reduction or premium that bends the line, so there are no spikes.
It steps up to 34.5% at $59,080 (the federal 20.5% bracket). From there it falls as Employment Insurance and the Canada Pension Plan stop. It reaches 36.5% from $61,780 (Alberta’s 10% bracket), then 35% at $68,900, 33.5% at $74,600 and 30.5% at $85,000.
After that it only climbs: 36% from $118,180, then 38%, 41.5%, 42.5%, 43.5%, 47% from $259,610, and 48% from $371,350, where the line is straight.

yearTotal
This chart adds income tax, Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance, then takes off benefits paid to you when you file. At low salaries the total is below zero. It is $15,893 at $70,000, and $25,542 at $100,000.
The line bends upward at each new bracket, then goes straight. From $371,350 up, every extra dollar loses the top rate, 47%, so each extra $10,000 of salary adds $4,700. At $500,000 the total is $201,745."
`;

