exports[`the text of the hub charts > writes every province and chart as one reviewed text, so a change in wording or in a figure shows in review > ca-mb 1`] = `
"takeHome
Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance together stop growing at $85,000, at most $5,770 a year. Employment Insurance stops growing at about $68,900, and the Canada Pension Plan at about $74,600. Above that, each extra dollar loses only income tax.
From $401,130 up, the line is straight: you keep 48.74% of every extra dollar. At $500,000 you keep 55.57% of your salary.

averageRate
Up to $2,000 the average is 0%. Employment Insurance starts just above $2,000 and counts for more as your salary rises, until the full rate applies by about $2,033. The average is about 1.6% from about $2,040.
Up to about $17,410 you pay no income tax, only Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance. The average is 5.5% at $10,000.
After that the average climbs: 15.9% at $30,000, 21.9% at $50,000, 25.9% at $70,000 and 28.6% at $100,000.
It climbs more slowly than the marginal rate, because each higher rate applies only to the dollars above its bracket. At $500,000 the average is 44.4%, while the top marginal rate is 51.5%.
Manitoba’s own tax starts before federal tax does, so the average is 8.5% at $20,000, more than in provinces whose own tax starts later.

marginalRate
Up to $2,000 the rate is 0%. From $2,000 to about $2,033 the rate is 100%, because the Employment Insurance counted rises by $1 for every $1 of salary, until it reaches about $33. After that it takes 1.63 cents of each extra dollar.
From $4,000 to about $17,410 the rate is 7.5%: Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance, with no income tax yet.
Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance take about 7.5 cents of each dollar. Employment Insurance stops at about $68,900, and the main Canada Pension Plan amount at $74,600. A second Canada Pension Plan tier takes 4 cents of each dollar from $74,600 to $85,000, so the rate falls less at $74,600 than at $85,000.
Manitoba’s own tax starts before the federal tax does. The rate is 18.5% from $17,410: Manitoba tax, Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance. It is 31.5% from $19,200, once federal tax joins in, then 30.5% from $23,140. The Manitoba Family Tax Benefit lowers your Manitoba tax and shrinks by 9 cents for every dollar of income. It is gone at about $23,140, so the rate falls by about 1 point there.
It steps up to 32.5% at $47,440 (Manitoba’s next bracket) and 39% at $59,080 (the federal 20.5% bracket), then falls as Employment Insurance and the Canada Pension Plan stop: 37.5% at $68,900, 36% at $74,600 and 33.5% at $85,000.
At $201,130 of salary it steps up about 1 point to 47.5%, and it reaches 51.3% from $259,610 to $401,130. Then it drops about 1 point, to 50.4%. The cause is the Manitoba basic personal amount, which shrinks to zero as net income goes from $200,000 to $400,000, so you lose a little tax saving on each extra dollar. The line is straight from $401,130.

yearTotal
This chart adds income tax, Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance, then takes off benefits paid to you when you file. At low salaries the total is below zero. It is $18,101 at $70,000, and $28,555 at $100,000.
The line bends upward at each new bracket, then goes straight. From $401,130 up, every extra dollar loses the top rate, 51.5%, so each extra $10,000 of salary adds $5,126. At $500,000 the total is $222,174."
`;

