The rate stays 20.5% (federal tax only) until about $25,100, because Newfoundland and Labrador’s low-income tax reduction removes the provincial tax at low incomes.
From $25,100 to $30,760 the rate is 44.5%, much higher than before. The reduction shrinks by 16 cents for every dollar of income above $24,191, so you start paying the provincial tax and lose the reduction at the same time. It is gone at about $30,770.
Once the reduction is gone the rate falls to 28.5% from $30,770. It steps up to 34.5% from $45,100 (the second bracket) and 40.5% from $59,080 (the federal 20.5% bracket), then down as pension and insurance stop. The top rate in this chart is 54%, from $286,450, where the line is straight. The form adds higher rates above about $564,000.

