exports[`the text of the hub charts > writes every province and chart as one reviewed text, so a change in wording or in a figure shows in review > ca-on 1`] = `
"takeHome
Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance together stop growing at $85,000, at most $5,770 a year. Employment Insurance stops growing at about $68,900, and the Canada Pension Plan at about $74,600. Above that, each extra dollar loses only income tax.
From $259,610 up, the line is straight: you keep 46.47% of every extra dollar. At $500,000 you keep 54.62% of your salary.

averageRate
Up to $2,000 the average is 0%. Employment Insurance starts just above $2,000 and counts for more as your salary rises, until the full rate applies by about $2,033. The average is about 1.6% from about $2,040.
Up to about $19,200 you pay no income tax, only Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance. The average is 5.5% at $10,000 and 7.1% at $20,000.
After that the average climbs: 12.5% at $30,000, 19.7% at $50,000, 23.2% at $70,000 and 25.8% at $100,000.
It climbs more slowly than the marginal rate, because each higher rate applies only to the dollars above its bracket. At $500,000 the average is 45.4%, while the top marginal rate is 53.5%.

marginalRate
Up to $2,000 the rate is 0%. From $2,000 to about $2,033 the rate is 100%, because the Employment Insurance counted rises by $1 for every $1 of salary, until it reaches about $33. After that it takes 1.63 cents of each extra dollar.
From $4,000 to about $19,200 the rate is 7.5%: Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance, with no income tax yet. It is 20.5% from $19,200, when federal income tax starts.
Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance take about 7.5 cents of each dollar. Employment Insurance stops at about $68,900, and the main Canada Pension Plan amount at $74,600. A second Canada Pension Plan tier takes 4 cents of each dollar from $74,600 to $85,000, so the rate falls less at $74,600 than at $85,000.
From $20,170 to $25,210 of salary the Ontario Health Premium adds 6 cents for every dollar, up to $300 in all. The rate is 26.5%, then 20.5% again.
From $32,800 to $38,850 the rate is 30% or more, higher than the 20.5% before it. The Ontario Low-income Individuals and Families Tax (LIFT) Credit lowers your Ontario tax. It shrinks by 5 cents for every dollar above $32,500, so more of each extra dollar goes to Ontario tax.
From $36,000 to $38,500 of taxable income the Ontario Health Premium adds 6 cents more, lifting the rate to 36%. LIFT keeps shrinking until about $50,470, and the rate falls to 25% once it is gone.
Three short spikes push the rate above the top rate of 53.5%: 54.9% from $48,450 to $49,050, 59.1% from $72,700 to $73,290, and 73.3% from $201,130 to $201,720 of salary. Each one is a step in the Ontario Health Premium. Across a band about $600 wide, the premium grows by 25 cents for every extra dollar, adding $150 in all. The rate falls back as soon as the band ends.
Ontario’s surtax raises the rate twice. A surtax is an extra tax on top of your Ontario tax, charged once that tax passes a set amount. It adds 20 cents for every dollar of Ontario tax above $5,818, and another 36 cents above $7,446. The rate rises to 31.5% at $98,590, and to 38% at $115,040.
From $259,610 of salary the line is straight: the rate stops rising, and every extra dollar loses the top rate, 53.5%.

yearTotal
This chart adds income tax, Canada Pension Plan and Employment Insurance, then takes off benefits paid to you when you file. At low salaries the total is below zero. It is $16,214 at $70,000, and $25,794 at $100,000.
The line bends upward at each new bracket, then goes straight. From $259,610 up, every extra dollar loses the top rate, 53.5%, so each extra $10,000 of salary adds $5,353. At $500,000 the total is $226,922."
`;

